  "summary": "Recent genomic studies across six bacterial pathogens test whether standardized typing tracks resistance and virulence at strain level.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is genome-resolved surveillance of bacterial pathogens, in which typing schemes and mobile-element context are applied to strain-level questions. An 846-locus core-genome MLST schema for Chlamydia trachomatis is benchmarked against core-SNP and PubMLST approaches. Comparative work on Streptococcus suis and S. parasuis reports mobile-element enrichment of antimicrobial resistance genes without detectable same-element colocalization with virulence-associated genes within stable species boundaries. Pan-genome analysis of 120 ESKAPE genomes links accessory content to resistance and virulence burden. A preprint on diarrheagenic E. coli in northern Ecuador reports rapid pathotype switching at genomovar resolution, which bears on the reliability of discrete virulence-gene diagnostics. Other items address serotype assignment directly (Shigella), host-specific resistance trends (Staphylococcus aureus), policy-linked transmission (Vibrio parahaemolyticus), and ecological structuring (Leptospira). The distinctive advance is standardized typing paired with mobile-element context rather than single-marker detection.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
